{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And they fell upon their faces.</b> To pray. And from here [is derived the law of] falling on the face in prayer. Know that the matter of falling on the face in prayer carries three intentions: the first, for awe of the Shekhinah; the second, to show distress and self-abasement; and the third, to show the suspension of one's senses and the nullification of one's faculties. <b>The first</b>, for awe of the Shekhinah, is so that one may thereby clothe himself in shame and modesty, for covering the face is among the ways of humility and shame; and since the intention of the one who prays is that the Shekhinah is before him, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 16:8): ״שויתי ה' לנגדי תמיד״ — therefore they ordained in the arrangement of prayer the covering of the face, and all for fear of HaShem, blessed be He, in the manner of what is written concerning Moses (Exodus 3:6): ״ויסתר משה פניו כי ירא מהביט אל האלהים״. And this expression of falling on the face the Sages, of blessed memory, also mentioned concerning the holy living creatures, and thus they expounded: the holy living creatures fall on their faces so that they do not gaze in the presence of the Shekhinah, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 1:14): ״והחיות רצוא ושוב״ — that is to say, they are not permitted to linger in that running and to gaze upward beyond the firmament stretched over their heads because of the dread of the Shekhinah; therefore it immediately says ושוב — meaning they hasten to turn back their heads from beneath the firmament, and this is (ibid.) ״כמראה הבזק״, and it did not say כמראה ברק, because the lightning-flash (ברק) shoots downward whereas the flash (בזק) rises and falls, and the word בזק is from the root of זיקות; and the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: ״כמראה הבזק״ — like this that kindles olive-residue in a furnace, for when one places the refuse of olives in the furnace, immediately the flame rises in the oven and the blaze rises and falls, and this is the meaning of רצוא ושוב. <br><b>And the view</b> of Jonathan regarding רצוא ושוב is one matter, for he translated: וברייתא חזרן ומקפן ית עלמא ותיבן בריא חדא וקבילין כחיזו ברקא — his intention is to say that when they are dispatched they encompass the world to the four winds, since each living creature has four faces, one for each direction, and afterward they return as a single creature upon completing their mission, for so you find in the second chariot (Ezekiel 1:10): ״היא החיה אשר ראיתי״ and so forth. And likewise the Sages, of blessed memory, proved the intention of the feet, which is also among the arrangements of prayer, from the verse (Ezekiel 1:7): ״ורגליהם רגל ישרה״, said of the holy living creatures." "<b>The second</b>, to show distress and self-abasement. It is that the one who falls on his face is distressed and abased, and self-abasement is among the essential principles of repentance, and then his prayer is accepted and the Holy One, blessed be He, is mindful of his distress and fulfills his request. And thus the Sages, of blessed memory, said concerning the righteous man who suffers: חלש דעתיה ואתא מטרא — his mind was troubled and rain came; אכסיף ואתא מטרא — he was ashamed and rain came. <br><b>And similar</b> to this they said in Bava Metzia, end of the chapter הזהב: the wife of R. Eliezer, who was the sister of Rabban Gamliel — and so forth. From that incident onward, she would not let R. Eliezer fall on his face in supplication, the reason being lest he suffer, and out of his suffering, self-abasement, and humiliation he might punish Rabban Gamliel her brother, who was head of the academy and had insulted him through his counsel. From this you learn that this lady was not afraid that he would punish him through his prayer, but only through falling on the face. And there the episode concludes that through falling on the face he did punish him: she was not careful one day which she thought was Rosh Chodesh and did not take care to observe it on that day, and then he fell on his face, and she heard a sound of a tanna proclaiming in the academy of Rabban Gamliel her brother, and she said to him: קטלתיה לאחי — you have killed my brother." "<b>The third</b>, to show the suspension of his senses and the nullification of his faculties. It is that the one who falls on his face covers his eyes and closes his mouth, and he agrees in his thought that he does not see his own harm or benefit and does not know his way or his affairs, and it is not in his power to achieve his desire unless the Holy One, blessed be He, grants it through him, in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 10:23): ״כי לא לאדם דרכו״ — as if his faculties were nullified and suspended from attaining what he seeks, and his eyes and lips are closed so that he cannot see or speak except through the expression of the will of HaShem, may His Name be blessed, and this parallels what one agrees in his heart when he intends his feet in prayer as though his feet were bound — his desire is not dependent on himself." "<b>And behold,</b> the nations show this same intention by clasping the hands in supplication, and they themselves do not know why they became habituated to do so. And the reason for this is to show the nullification of one's own power, as though his hands were bound and he surrenders himself to the One to whom he pleads; and since the movement of the feet is greater than the movement of the hands for bringing one's benefit and warding off one's harm, therefore we practice the intention of the feet and not the hands." "<b>And in the name</b> of our master R. Hai Gaon, of blessed memory, it is fitting for a person to fall on his face on the left side, and he gave a reason for this: so as to subjugate through falling on the face the hand that alludes to freedom on the night of Passover. <br>And behold, the prayer that Moses prayed in falling on the face is what he said: <b>O El, Elohim of the spirits for all flesh.</b> Its meaning is: do not be severe so as to be able to destroy them in an instant; rather, You are the Elohim of the spirits, and the spirits are in Your hand, and You know who has sinned against You and who has purged his heart and been cleansed of his sin, and Your measure is not like the measure of flesh and blood — a king of flesh and blood, if part of a province offends him, takes vengeance on all of them because he does not know who the sinner is, but You test hearts and kidneys. <b>Shall one man sin,</b> — this is Korah and his congregation, who assembled against Moses and against Aaron all the congregation. <b>And will You be wrathful against all the congregation?</b> — he and his congregation caused them to sin, and the sin of the many is dependent on them. And this prayer of Moses and Aaron in falling on the face was brief; and so too Moses when he prayed for Miriam shortened his prayer and said: ״אל נא רפא נא לה״ — there he recounted the praise of the Creator by saying אל and prayed in the language of supplication by saying נא, but here he prolonged more to recount His praise, as the Sages, of blessed memory, said: a person should always recount the praise of the Holy One, blessed be He, and afterward ask for his needs; and therefore they ordained in the prayer אבות and גבורות and קדושת השם, and afterward the middle blessings, which are the asking of needs." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that Scripture says אל אלהי הרוחות — that He is the Elohim of the angels, for they are spirits; as it is written (Daniel 2:11): ״להן אלהין די מדרהון עם בשרא לא איתוהי״ — and He is also Elohim to all flesh. The intent is: You are HaShem of the supernal beings and the lower beings, and it is fitting for You to render judgment justly — shall one man sin and against the whole congregation will You be wrathful?" "<b>Furthermore,</b> it appears to me to explain that Moses and Aaron called Him Elohei ha-ruchot just as the patriarchs called Him (Genesis 24:3) Elohei ha-shamayim ve-Elohei ha-aretz, the intent being that He is the Elohim who rules over the four winds of the world, in the manner of what Scripture says (Exodus 8:18): ״אני ה׳ בקרב הארץ״ — for He, exalted be He, is like a king whose palace is in the middle of the city, watching over its four winds. And since the patriarchs praised Him as Elohei ha-shamayim ve-Elohei ha-aretz, which are two extremes, above and below, Moses wished to praise Him with the four winds of the world in order to complete the praise in six extremes, for He, exalted be He, rules over all and there is none to hinder Him, and who can say to Him, what are You doing?"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {0 "Source lists the Psalm citation as (תהילים ט״ז:ח׳); Ezekiel citations written as (יחזקאל א׳:י״ד) and (שם י) and (שם א); all normalized to standard form.", 1 "The Talmudic Aramaic quotations (חלש דעתיה ואתא מטרא etc.) and the final line (קטלתיה לאחי) are left untranslated as they are embedded as quoted speech, per the Hebrew-script rule."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:41.120474428Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}